cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >
  If you use this benchmark or build on these findings, please cite
  the paper as described below.

title: >
  Metric Unreliability in Multimodal Machine Unlearning:
  A Systematic Analysis and Principled Unified Score

authors:
  - name: "Anonymous"
    affiliation: "Anonymous Institution"

version: "1.0.0"
date-released: "2026"

abstract: >
  Five widely-used metrics for evaluating multimodal machine unlearning
  (FA, RA, MIA, AD, JS) produce contradictory method rankings in a
  systematic and predictable way. Kendall's tau analysis over 36 unlearned
  LLaVA-1.5-7B models reveals two anti-correlated clusters — output-surface
  metrics (FA, RA, MIA) vs oracle-alignment metrics (AD, JS) — with
  tau_FA,AD = -0.26, replicated on BLIP-2 OPT-2.7B. Metric agreement is
  lower in multimodal VQA settings (mean tau = 0.086) than unimodal
  classification (mean tau = 0.158). We identify Knowledge Recoverability
  (KR) as the aspect missed by all five metrics — 23/24 FA=0 samples still
  leaked under structured probing (96%). The Unified Quality Score (UQS)
  provides a principled aggregation of measurable aspects weighted by
  empirical Spearman correlation with a retrained oracle model.

keywords:
  - machine unlearning
  - multimodal learning
  - visual question answering
  - evaluation benchmarks
  - metric reliability
  - knowledge recoverability
  - LLaVA
  - BLIP-2
  - GDPR
  - privacy

